WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.790
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.720
With the power of modern computer graphics
and machine learning techniques, we are now

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:15.480
able to teach virtual humanoids to walk, sit,
manipulate objects, and we can even make up

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.830
new creature types and teach them new tricks…if
we are patient enough, that is.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.279
But, even with all this knowledge, we are
not done yet, are we?

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.099
Should we just shut down all the research
facilities, because there is nothing else

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:31.099
to do?

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:36.000
Well, if you have spent any amount of time
watching Two Minute Papers, you know that

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.160
the answer is, of course not!

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.340
There is so much to do I don’t even know
where to start!

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:47.010
For instance, let’s consider the case of
deformable simulations.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.470
Not so long ago, we talked about Yuanming
Hu’s amazing paper with which, we can engage

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.840
in the favorite pastime of a computer graphics
researcher, which is, of course, destroying

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.210
virtual objects in a spectacular manner.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:06.080
It can also create remarkably accurate jello
simulations, where we can even choose our

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.960
physical parameters.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.009
Here you see how we can drop in blocks of
different densities into the jello, and as

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:16.680
a result, they sink in deeper and deeper.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.899
Amazing.

00:01:17.899 --> 00:01:22.939
However, note that this is not for real-time
applications and computer games because the

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:29.619
execution time is measured not in frames per
second, but in seconds per frame.

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:35.049
If we are looking for somewhat coarse results,
but in real time, we have covered a paper

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:41.259
approximately 300 episodes ago, which performed
something that is called a Reduced Deformable

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:42.509
Simulation.

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:45.630
Leave a comment if you were already a Fellow
Scholar back then!

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.359
This technique could be trained on a number
of different representative cases, which,

00:01:50.359 --> 00:01:55.259
in computer graphics research, is often referred
to as precomputation, which means that we

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:01.710
have to do a ton of work before starting a
task, but only once, and then, all our subsequent

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.090
simulations can be sped up.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:09.759
Kind of like a student studying before an
exam, so when the exam itself happens, the

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.819
student, in the ideal case, will know exactly
what to do.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:18.040
Imagine trying to learn the whole subject
during the exam!

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.290
Note that this training in this technique
is not the same kind of training we are used

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:28.129
to see with neural networks, and its generalization
capabilities were limited, meaning that if

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:33.069
we strayed too far from the training examples,
the algorithm did not work so reliably.

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:37.590
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
new method contains a ton of optimizations,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:43.970
runs on your graphics card, and hence, can
perform these deformable simulations at close

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.110
to 40 frames per second.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.230
And in the following examples in a moment,
you will see something even better.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:55.590
A killer advantage of this method is that
this is also scalable.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.709
This means that the resolution of the object
geometry can be changed around, here, the

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:05.340
upper left is a coarse version of the object,
where the lower right is the most refined

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.900
version of it.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.950
Of course, the number of frames we can put
out per second depends a great deal on the

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:17.269
resolution of this geometry, and if you have
a look, this looks very close to the one below

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:23.659
it, but is still more than 3 to 6 times faster
than real time.

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:24.659
Wow.

00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:32.480
And whenever we are dealing with collisions,
lots of amazing details appear.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.829
Just look at this!

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:38.989
Let’s look at a little more formal measurement
of the scalability of this method.

00:03:38.989 --> 00:03:44.079
Note that this is a log-log plot, since the
number of tetrahedra used for the geometry

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:48.040
and the execution time spans many orders of
magnitude.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.920
In other words, we can see how it works from
the coarsest piece of geometry to the most

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.290
detailed models we can throw at it.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.749
If we look at something like this, we are
hoping that the lines are not too steep, which

00:03:59.749 --> 00:04:03.670
is the case for both the memory and execution
timings.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:09.099
So, finally, real-time deformable simulations,
here we come!

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:10.969
What a time to be alive!

